!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/32/ltr8/http/meta-http-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta-kernel.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta.tn"
@doc:"""
  A meta layer adding HTTP operations to the type system: a schema governed by this one describes an
  HTTP API, and its request and response payloads are type references the compiler resolves.

  This is the design picked out of four (sketch/README.md compares them). What it has that a data
  document cannot is the reference: `body: order` is checked when the description loads, where a data
  description spells "order" as a string and needs its own resolver -- forty lines that reimplemented
  an upstream namespace bug twice before they were right.

  `~data &` is the base kind for an entry that is not a type: an operation describes an endpoint,
  and no data value ever has one as its type. Before that kind existed this said `~top &`, which
  resolved but could not be registered, because registering a constructor demanded a value-reader
  factory -- a reader for data of that type, which an operation can never have.

  `~` is still required, and the reason is worth stating: it is the permission for a SCHEMA to write
  `!operation { ... }` (§3.3.1/§5.6), not a claim that the thing is a type. Without it the
  declaration resolves and a schema applying it is told the name does not resolve to a constructor.

  request/response are `type_ref`, not `type_name` or text, and that is what makes the references
  real: a Data body hands its references to the linker, so a name that resolves to nothing is an
  author error at schema load. `type_name` has no such path.

  Governed directly by the meta-kernel, which is not a style choice: declaring a constructor is
  permitted only in a schema whose own !!meta is the kernel. So this is a SIBLING of meta.tn, and
  imports it for the vocabulary its own declarations use.
  """
{
  http_method => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

  @doc:"Refined, so 42 is not a status."
  status_code => !integer ^ { min: 100  max: 599 }

  parameter_location => !enum [PATH QUERY HEADER]

  @doc:"""
    A parameter carried outside the body. `type` names a scalar and nothing here enforces that -- a URL
    segment cannot carry a record, and the type system has no way to say so.

    `description` is a field rather than an @doc because a parameter is a VALUE inside an operation's
    payload, not a schema entry, so there is nothing for an annotation to attach to. Same for response.
    """
  parameter => {
    name:        text
    in:          parameter_location
    type:        type_ref
    required:    boolean
    description: text?
  }

  @doc:"One response. `body` is absent for a 204, or a document served as bytes."
  response => {
    status:      status_code
    body:        type_ref?
    description: text?
  }

  @doc:"""
    One operation. A list of responses rather than a map keyed by status: a response is likely to
    grow members beyond its body, and a map-typed slot cannot hold a template parameter, which would
    foreclose templated operations before they are tried.

    There is no `description` field: an operation IS a schema entry, so @doc carries its long form and
    a reader gets it back from the entry's annotations. `summary` is the short form OpenAPI keeps
    beside the long one.

    Worth knowing, because it cost a wrongly-filed upstream item: a schema entry has TWO annotation
    positions and they land in different places. `@doc:"..." op => ...` annotates the ENTRY and is read
    from the entries map's own annotations; `op => @doc:"..." { ... }` annotates the DEFINITION and is
    read from the TypeDefinition. Both are retained. Looking only at the second and concluding @doc was
    dropped is the mistake.

    The entry's NAME is the operationId, and unlike OpenAPI's it lives in a namespace with a collision
    rule, so two operations cannot quietly share one.

    Deliberately absent, each a decision rather than an oversight: security schemes, response headers,
    links, callbacks, examples, tags, servers, and multi-media-type content negotiation. Adding one is
    meta-http-2.tn under §10 once this is published, never an edit.
    """
  operation => ~data & {
    method:      http_method
    path:        text
    summary:     text?
    deprecated:  boolean?
    parameters:  [parameter]
    request:     type_ref?
    responses:   [response]
  }
}
